WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.550
The greatest common divisor of a and b equals the greatest common divisor of b and a modulo b.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:14.297
Each modulo operation shrinks the smaller argument by at least half every two steps.

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:20.080
Use Python's compiled math.gcd and math.lcm functions for maximum speed.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:29.431
The Extended Euclidean Algorithm tracks coefficients x and y such that a times x plus b times y equals gcd(a, b).

00:00:29.431 --> 00:00:37.080
Use an iterative tuple swap in Python to avoid recursion depth issues on sixty-four-bit inputs.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:45.533
Linear Diophantine equations have integer solutions if and only if the right-hand side is divisible by g.

00:00:45.792 --> 00:00:53.552
An integer a has a multiplicative inverse modulo m exactly when a and m are coprime.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:01:00.204
Extended Euclid finds this inverse even when the modulus m is composite.

00:01:00.204 --> 00:01:07.872
Built into Python 3.8 and later, pow(a, -1, m) runs Extended Euclid in fast C code.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:16.578
The Chinese Remainder Theorem merges two modular equations into a single congruence modulo the least common multiple of their moduli.

00:01:16.578 --> 00:01:24.205
When the difference of remainders is divisible by gcd(m1, m2), Extended Euclid locates the merged remainder in one step.

00:01:24.205 --> 00:01:28.336
Fold a list of congruences pairwise to solve systems of any size.
